Syed Ali Zaidi, 28, Quetta; Second room; Go to;

THIRD ROOM

As the jingle signifying the end of the news report plays you turn
off the radio. Your eyes are heavy but you promise yourself a hot
cup of tea and a long sleep just as soon as you reach home.  There
were the usual cases in the ER last night, road traffic casualties,
a businessman with a heart attack and a suicide attempt. All patched
up and fairly good as new by the time you signed off in the morning. 

The news though, that was troubling.A part of your mind was
worriedly gnawing at the report, turning it over and over, thiking
about the consequences and the implications.  Conditions were
terrible in pakistan these days.  It was best to just get out of the
country alive, while one still could. But you knew that wasn’t an
option for you. 

It wasn;t that a doctor of your standing couldn't find a position
abroad, it was simply a deep streak of patriotism and a sense of
obstinacy that kept you sticking here.  And your mother of course.
Adjusting to new ways would be difficult for her. Speaking of which,
you had to pick her up today.

You take the turning back to the school near your house and wait for
your mother as the bell inside rings and the children come flooding
out. 


FOURTH ROOM

"Assalam-alaikum Beta", She said affectionately, as she sat down in
the car. "You seem really tired today."

"Walaikum Salam Maa", You reply. She was partly correct. You were
tired, but you were also worried. However, it made no sense to worry
her too. 

You were the only child of your mother, who was widowed while you
were still eight years old. For the past twenty years, she had spent
her days teaching at a local school and her veings making sure that
you stayed abreast of your class. 

It was not an easy job. She had to navigate the treacherous waters
of the society with a constant vigilence, lest people point fingers
at her. Her struggle bore fruit, and you became a doctor, like she
had always dreamed of. She still taught, out of love for the job and
a desire to stay active.  And she still fussed over you as she did
when you were a child. You were the centre of all her hopes and
dreams and efforts. 



FIFTH ROOM

The next day you arrive at the hospital to find a note from the
supervisor.  You had been called to his office.  While you are
waiting outside, you see your fellow resident, a young man who
boasted more of resources than skills at doctoring, leave the room
with a smarmy smile on his face.  

As you enter the office, the supervisor is hastily shoving a an
envelope into a drawer at his desk and closing it. You wait until he
straightens up.  He seems more red in the face than usual, and his
demeanour, never very encouraging or open in the first place, seems
colder.  

He shuffles around papers on his desk, ignoring you long enough to
make you uncomfortable. When you clear your throat, he looks up at
you abruptly. 

"Dr Zaidi, we have decided not to take you on for the senior post
graduate position this year (I don't know what to fill here, put
anything that sounds plausible)."

"Based on your performance, we feel it would be best if you
continued a year more in senior house officer (???).  When you have
enough experience you may be promoted to the post."

You feel your heart sink, and anger rise up. You debate asking him
who HAD gotten the position.

Ask/don't ask

"The position has been given to Dr Raza. He is a fine young
gentleman who has shown persuasively excellent performance
throughout."

No wonder the fellow resident looked so smarmy. He must have pulled
some strings, or pulled out his wallet anyhow. You feel the anger
turning to rage, and exit the room before you can make a scene.

BAck in the ward, you A) decide to write a letter to the HOD/Medical
director (I don't know this stuff) B) decide to do nothing



SIXTH ROOM

One week later. 


It is evening, and you have just woken up from confused dreams. Your
mother is standing over you, shaking you awake gently. 

"Beta, there is news of a blast in --- Imambargah. The victims will
be arriving in the hospital any time soon. You ought to be there."

You feel energy suffuse into your limbs, clearing away the
sleepiness and you get up and get dressed hurriedly, hugging your
mother on the way out.  You knew it broke her heart to disturb your
rest, but she knew how much it meant to you to help people, and
after  a blast the hospital needed as many hands as it could get.
You leave quickly. 



SEVENTH ROOM


One month later. 


You are heading back from evening duty one night. You have only just
noticed a van that seems to be tailing you and begun to feel
suspicious when it swerves to the side and in front of you, forcing
you to stop.  Four men step out. 

They come close to your car. One of them, wearing a black cloth over
the lower half of his face, taps the window of your car with his
gun. Open up, he signs. Terrified, you do so. He roughly pulls you
out, into the ditch at the side of the road. 

Two of the men push you onto your knees. They shout at you to put
your hands behind your head. You beg them to take anything, the car,
the mobile phone, the measly amount of money in your wallet. One of
them rifles your pockets, but they are not looking for money. The
check your ID card.

You are Dr ali Zaidi? Shi’a?

As the gun cocks in front of your head, you realize what they were
really after. You go cold. The last noise you hear is the bullet
exploding from the barrel, the last thought you think is of your
mother, waiting late into the night, while the lovingly cooked food
grows cold on the dinner table. 
